WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
When Amy looks herself up at the library,&nbsp;
she discovers that a website has been set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:06.640
up in an effort to find her.&nbsp;
The URL is findamazingamy.com,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:10.240
and if you visit that site it takes you to&nbsp;
the studio’s website, but if you look at an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.640
archived version of the page from 2014 when the&nbsp;
movie came out, you get something a little more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:15.431
interesting.

00:00:15.431 --> 00:00:18.240
And it seems that it’s not&nbsp;
too different from the main screen on the Blu-ray.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:22.560
However there are many other webpages, TV&nbsp;
screens, and billboards we can scrape for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:27.120
information as well as other Things You might have&nbsp;
Missed, so stick around to the end of this video.

00:00:27.703 --> 00:00:30.184
This video is sponsored by Hunt a Killer.

00:00:34.719 --> 00:00:35.872
“But CZ,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.872 --> 00:00:38.640
Gone Girl isn’t a horror movie, you’re&nbsp;
not allowed to cover it.”&nbsp;

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.840
OK, if Gone Girl isn’t a horror movie,&nbsp;
explain why I can’t show any of this scene.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.800
So with that settled, let’s&nbsp;
start looking for clues.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:57.840
“Well, we have our first clue.”

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.640
When Nick enters the bar on the morning of Amy’s&nbsp;
disappearance, he’s holding the game, Mastermind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.480
In the novel, Nick and Margot had this thing where&nbsp;
they’d bring over old board games and toys from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:16.320
their childhood home to help pass the time at&nbsp;
the bar. Although we don’t know it yet, perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:21.200
Mastermind is symbolic of his wife masterminding&nbsp;
a plan to get back at him for his infidelity.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.320
There are other games and books in&nbsp;
the stack that seem to represent things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.600
that would eventually happen in&nbsp;
the story. There’s Emergency!

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:30.756
Widowmaker…

00:01:30.756 --> 00:01:33.338
“Missouri has the death penalty.”

00:01:33.338 --> 00:01:34.573
Sex escort.

00:01:34.573 --> 00:01:37.035
“I don't know I'd have to check my red panty inventory.”

00:01:37.035 --> 00:01:37.724
Jailbait…

00:01:37.724 --> 00:01:38.741
“How old is she?”

00:01:38.741 --> 00:01:40.580
“She’s in her early 20s.”

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:41.818
Let’s Make A Deal…

00:01:41.818 --> 00:01:45.352
“This is why I&nbsp;
have a $100,000 retainer, I win the unwinnable cases.”

00:01:45.352 --> 00:01:46.722
“A hundred thousand&nbsp;
dollars?”

00:01:46.722 --> 00:01:48.080
“We’ll figure something out.”

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:49.040
The Hot Seat…

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:56.080
*sits* “Nick Dunn, you’re probably the most&nbsp;
***ed man in America right now.” “I probably am.”

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:00.320
There’s also a miniature Tom Sawyer riverboat,&nbsp;
to go along with the journal page about when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:04.160
Amy first met Nick and he told her he’s&nbsp;
from the childhood home of Mark Twain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:09.520
the author who created Tom Sawyer and Huckleberry&nbsp;
Finn. Tom Sawyer famously attended his own funeral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.960
after his family thought him to be dead,&nbsp;
which is nearly what happens with Amy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.800
watching her family search for her after&nbsp;
her disappearance and presumed death.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.320
When Nick doesn’t feel like talking,&nbsp;
his sister offers to distract him with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:24.000
excruciatingly mundane stories. It&nbsp;
isn’t ever mentioned in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.400
but in the book, telling these stories is a game&nbsp;
that Nick and Go play, inspired by their mom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.320
“who had a habit of telling such&nbsp;
outrageously mundane, endless stories.”

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:35.760
There are other instances of sort of inside&nbsp;
jokes for those who have read the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:37.840
like in the scene where Nick first meets Amy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:41.360
asks “who are you?” and she gives him&nbsp;
a variety of multiple choice answers.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:50.640
“A. I am an award—winning scrimshander. B. I&nbsp;
am a moderately influential warlord. C. I write&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:52.400
personality quizzes for magazines.”

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:53.760
“I’m gonna go with C.”

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.080
When we’re reading through&nbsp;
Amy’s diary entries in the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.120
this is something she does all&nbsp;
the time with her audience as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:02.560
“what do you think I did?” or “one of&nbsp;
the following is what actually happened.”

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:05.760
By the way, when we see Amy writing&nbsp;
the journal entries, you may notice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:09.840
all of the different pens she used, evidence&nbsp;
that she’s faking the entire journal by using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:13.200
different pens to make it look like the&nbsp;
entries were written on different days.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:17.120
Then there’s the Ellen Abbott segment where&nbsp;
the prominent talk show host is analysing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:20.960
Nick’s life and makes the suggestion that&nbsp;
he and his sister are “disturbingly close.”

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:25.600
“I didn’t use the I-word, I said&nbsp;
you two were extremely close.”

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:29.520
Ellen never makes this insinuation in the book,&nbsp;
but rather, it was a rumor that went around about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:33.440
Nick and Go when they were in high school, a part&nbsp;
of Nick’s life that is never covered in the movie.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:36.960
There are a ton of nods to the book in&nbsp;
this scene where police discover Amy’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:40.400
diary. Detective Boney flips through the&nbsp;
pages and if you pause you can see little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:44.560
snippets of Amy's writing. It seems as if&nbsp;
the ENTIRETY of Amy’s journal entries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:48.080
in the novel have been copied down&nbsp;
into this journal by hand and more.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:51.040
It reminds me of the last episode of Things&nbsp;
You Missed, where I covered another movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:54.960
directed by David Fincher, Zodiac, where I&nbsp;
mentioned that every newspaper on the set was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:59.760
that day’s paper printed in full. So I have&nbsp;
no doubt that Amy’s entire diary is here.&nbsp;

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:03.840
By the way, there is a connection to Zodiac&nbsp;
here. At the end of my Zodiac episode I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:07.760
mentioned that I’d be closing the Zodiac&nbsp;
case and moving on to the Amy Dunne case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:13.280
and Zodiac ended with this survivor, Mike&nbsp;
Mageau, identifying this suspect as The Zodiac.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:17.520
Well, as the missing person investigation gets&nbsp;
underway in Gone Girl, the same actor who played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:21.920
the young version of Mike Mageau also plays&nbsp;
the police officer who finds the first clue.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:25.440
There are other David Fincher movie&nbsp;
callbacks too. When Go is watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:29.680
Noelle Hawthorne’s interview on Ellen Abbott,&nbsp;
she’s drinking a glass of wine. The brand is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:33.040
Norton. Edward Norton plays the main&nbsp;
character from Fincher’s Fight Club.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:36.960
Side note: strange choice of beverage&nbsp;
to accompany your cereal and milk.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:40.240
In the book, Amy takes out&nbsp;
Desi with a kitchen knife. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:44.720
why in the movie has it been changed to a&nbsp;
box cutter? The only reason I can think of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:48.800
is to reference the most iconic&nbsp;
scene of David Fincher’s Se7en.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.760
And finally, while Detective&nbsp;
Boney is reading the Amy diary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:56.400
her partner asks, “you reading that&nbsp;
diary again? You know how it ends?”

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:00.880
...to which she replies, “it interests me,”&nbsp;
the same exact line used by a character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:04.000
named Lisbeth when she goes through&nbsp;
some documents related to a murder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:07.360
case in Fincher’s previous movie,&nbsp;
The Girl With The Dragon Tattoo.

00:05:08.632 --> 00:05:11.915
"Why would a young lady like you want to know about such an awful m*rder?"

00:05:11.915 --> 00:05:12.720
"It interests me."

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:16.000
Knowing Fincher, everything he does&nbsp;
is very deliberate, and we can look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:21.071
to the background details of many other Gone&nbsp;
Girl scenes for even more Things You Missed.

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:28.240
The season we've been waiting&nbsp;
for all year has finally arrived,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:31.520
and there's been a lot of disappointment in&nbsp;
the horror community because haunted houses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:34.240
Halloween parties and horror movie&nbsp;
premieres have all fallen victim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:38.400
to COVID-19. Many of us are left wondering how&nbsp;
we'll celebrate our favorite holiday this year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:41.920
and one of the coolest things I've found&nbsp;
is Hunt a Killer's new Blair Witch game.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:45.440
It's basically a subscription box that puts&nbsp;
you in the center of a new horror mystery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:48.560
taking place in the universe of one of the&nbsp;
greatest found-footage movies of all time:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:52.800
The Blair Witch Project. Each season consists&nbsp;
of 6 episodes. When you open the box you'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:56.400
receive your case file, which consists of&nbsp;
realistic evidence, we're talking journals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:59.680
police reports, maps and other documents&nbsp;
that will help you solve the mystery of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:03.280
Black Hills Forest. If I could compare to&nbsp;
anything, I'd say it really has the feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:07.200
of an escape room. It's very hands on, and it&nbsp;
makes you a character in the story. There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:11.040
certain parts where you make a revelation,&nbsp;
that's genuinely just creepy and unsettling.

